{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "am-04-mixing-existing-mixtures-strengths",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Mixing Existing Mixtures and Strengths",
    "description": "Mix prepared solutions by converting each source mixture into the fraction or percentage of one tracked component.",
    "teachingApproach": "Choose one component—milk, spirit, alcohol—and express every source mixture as a fraction or percentage of that same component. Then use alligation on those strengths. Progress from direct percentages to ratio-to-fraction conversion, target-ratio questions, splitting a fixed final quantity, and replacing part of a strong solution with a weaker one.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 29,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 21, Alligation or Mixture. Approved scope: Teach how to convert a ratio such as milk : water = 4 : 3 into a milk fraction 4/7 or percentage before mixing two prepared solutions. Apply alligation directly to concentration, alcohol strength, milk fraction, or spirit fraction. Cover finding the ratio in which two mixtures must be combined for a target composition, splitting a required total quantity between two source mixtures, and replacing part of a stronger solution with a weaker solution to reach a target strength. Learners should track one chosen component consistently; switching from milk fraction to water fraction mid-solution is a common source of errors."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's mix mixtures, not just pure ingredients.\n\nOne can may be 80% milk and another 50% milk. To make a 60% mixture, we need to decide how much to take from each can. The same alligation idea works once every source is measured on one common scale.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Suppose mixture A is 60% milk and mixture B is 20% milk. We want 40% milk.\n\nTreat 60, 20, and 40 exactly like prices in alligation.\n\nA:B = (40−20):(60−40) = 20:20 = 1:1.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Why does this work?\n\nThe final milk percentage is a weighted average of the two source percentages.\n\nAlligation only needs two endpoint values and one target mean. The values do not have to be money.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
      "id": "q_1",
      "type": "CHOICE",
      "content": {
        "text": "In what ratio should 70% and 40% solutions be mixed to get 50%?",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "input": {
        "type": "CHOICE",
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "a",
            "label": "2 : 1"
          },
          {
            "id": "b",
